
  <sn><md>### Amino Acid
+++ read

* **Definition:** An amino acid is an organic compound containing both a basic **amino group $-NH_2$** and an acidic **carboxylic group $-COOH$**.

* **Basic Components:** Every standard amino acid contains 
   :::.row
   :::.col-12 .col-lg-8
   - an $\alpha$-carbon attached to an amino group, 
   - a carboxylic acid group, 
   - a hydrogen atom, and 
   - a variable side chain represented as the **$R$ group**.
   :::

   :::.col-12 .col-lg-4
   ![img](https://bio.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/8209/aminoacid.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=550&amp;height=543)
   :::
   :::

* **Standard Amino Acids:** Although over *300 amino acids* exist in nature, `20 standard amino acids` form proteins.

* **Zwitterion:** In ++aqueous environments++, amino acids exist in a stable dipolar form called a `zwitterion`. 

   In this state, the amino group accepts a proton $-NH_3^+$ while the carboxyl group donates a proton $-COO^-$, making the molecule `overall electrically neutral` despite possessing both positive and negative charges.
   ![img](https://sciencequery.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/zwitterion.png)

+++</md></sn>
